cb=source('certeau_loudun','BnF: La Possession de Loudun, texts selected/presented by Michel de Certeau (Julliard,1970).','https://catalogue.bnf.fr/ark:/12148/cb35167432w','Author/editor fields and1970 publication checked. Scientific editing/presentation of historical texts retained; volume body unread.',True)
cm=source('certeau_original','BnF, La société de consommation: selected bibliography (2024), de Certeau Arts de faire.','https://www.bnf.fr/sites/default/files/2024-09/Bibliographie_La_societe_de_consommation.pdf','National-library bibliography explicitly gives original1980 for L’invention du quotidien,1:Arts de faire; date metadata only.',True)
cw=source('certeau_writing','MSH Editions: Das Schreiben der Geschichte; original L’écriture de l’histoire (Gallimard,1975).','https://www.editions-msh.fr/livre/das-schreiben-der-geschichte/','Publisher explicitly identifies French original1975; later German translation is not dated as the original contribution. Metadata only.',True)
cl=source('certeau_life','Savoie Mont Blanc University LLSETI, Michel de Certeau centenary.','https://www.llseti.univ-smb.fr/web/llseti/1080-centenaire-michel-de-certeau.php','Read institutional opening biography: bornChambéry1925, diedParis1986. Life-year evidence only.',True)
bio('michel_de_certeau','Michel de Certeau joined historical research on religion and mysticism to questions about how histories are written and how people use cultural forms. La Possession de Loudun presents selected historical texts; L’écriture de l’histoire examines historical practice. In The Practice of Everyday Life, he investigates situated ways of operating within imposed systems. His account of users’ creative activities also questions explanations concerned chiefly with disciplinary power. The English preface distinguishes his volume from Luce Giard and Pierre Mayol’s companion work on dwelling and cooking.',[event(1970,'La Possession de Loudun — selected and presented historical texts',[cb],'Julliard1970; de Certeau is credited as scientific editor/presenter, not author of every historical document.'),event(1975,'L’écriture de l’histoire',[cw],'French original1975; later translations remain distinct.'),event(1980,'L’invention du quotidien,1: Arts de faire',[cp,cm],'French original1980; companion volume2Habiter,cuisiner is by LuceGiard and PierreMayol, as English preface ix explains.'),event(1984,'The Practice of Everyday Life — Steven Rendall’s English translation',[cp],'English preface dated26February1984; translation of Arts de faire, distinct from1980 French original.',kind='edition',track='editions')],[edge('practice','Situated ways of operating become objects of historical and cultural inquiry','English preface ix and General Introduction xi–xii examine relational practices and refuse an atomistic model of the user; this is a bounded methodological contribution.',[cp]),edge('consumption','Cultural consumption can transform the uses of imposed representations','General Introduction xii–xiv distinguishes representations and purchase/attendance statistics from what users make with cultural products. This does not establish that every consumer resists power.',[cp]),edge('foucault','Questions an account of discipline centred on the apparatus that produces it','General Introduction xiv–xv directly discusses Discipline and Punish and asks how users appropriate or evade disciplinary arrangements. This specific criticism preserves the acknowledged analytical debt.',[cp],'critique')],'Read English preface ix–x and General Introduction xi–xv via OCR of the later paperback scan; original French and body chapters not collated. Colonial generalizations in the introductory example are not adopted as established facts.',[('consumption','Examines how the use of cultural products may transform their meaning and operation.','The Practice of Everyday Life, English translation(1984),xii–xiv',[cp],1)],{cp:{'status':'supporting_page_checked','checked_on':DAY,'note':'Additional188review: reread English preface ix–x and General Introduction xi–xv via OCR of the same later paperback scan. Specific practices/consumption connections and Foucault critique use only those passages. Giard/Mayol companion-volume and Rendall translation credits retained; no body-chapter or original-language collation claimed.'}})
life('michel_de_certeau','deceased',[(cl,'Opening institutional biography','Explicit birth1925 and death1986.')],1925,1986,limits='Year precision follows the institutional witness; no fabricated day.')

# Charles Tilly.
tw=source('tilly_war','Charles Tilly, “War Making and State Making as Organized Crime,” in Bringing the State Back In, ed.Peter Evans,Dietrich Rueschemeyer,Theda Skocpol (Cambridge,1985),169–191.','https://www.bmartin.cc/pubs/19sd/refs/Tilly1985.pdf','Read printed169–172 of primary chapter scan. Argument is tentative and based on European state formation; Tilly rejects reading contemporary countries’ futures directly from Europe’s past. Editors retained. Rest of chapter unread.')
tl=source('tilly_life','Columbia University, “University Mourns Charles Tilly” (2008).','https://www.columbia.edu/cu/polisci/fac/newsarchive07-08/tilly/index.html','Read full institutional obituary, dates and selected works. BirthMay27,1929; deathApril29,2008. The memorial misdates ContentiousFrench elsewhere; its year is independently grounded in existing publisher-reviewed source.',True)
tc=source('tilly_coercion','Charles Tilly, Coercion, Capital, and European States,AD990–1990, original copyright/imprint (Blackwell,1990).','https://www.sscc.wisc.edu/soc/faculty/pages/wright/Soc924-2011/TillyCh1.pdf','Primary title/copyright page indexed: firstpublished1990 and original title990–1990. Later990–1992 revision is distinct. Chapter body not used as substantive evidence.',True)
bio('charles_tilly','Charles Tilly combined historical sociology with research on collective action, revolution and state formation. His studies range from counterrevolution in the Vendée to changing French political repertoires and comparisons across European states. In his essay on war making, coercion and extraction help explain the growth of state organizations, while resistance and bargaining constrain rulers. These are historically situated arguments; he explicitly rejects treating early modern Europe as a ready-made forecast for twentieth-century societies.',[event(1964,'The Vendée: A Sociological Analysis of the Counter-Revolution of1793',[tl],'Original1964 book identified in institutional selected works; body unread.'),event(1985,'War Making and State Making as Organized Crime',[tw],'Chapter in Bringing the State Back In; Evans,Rueschemeyer and Skocpol are editors, not coauthors of this chapter.'),event(1986,'The Contentious French',['tilly_contentious'],'Existing source independently identifies1986 original; full volume unread.'),event(1990,'Coercion, Capital, and European States,AD990–1990',[tc],'Original1990 title/date retained; later990–1992 revision not silently folded into first publication.')],[edge('military','War finance and organized coercion help shape European state formation','Primary chapter169–172 links warfare, resource extraction and capital accumulation; analogy with protection rackets is an argument, not a claim that every state is identical to a criminal organization.',[tw]),edge('historicalsociology','Coercion and bargaining are analysed as historically variable processes','Primary chapter169–170 says popular resistance could force rulers to concede protection and constraints, and rejects direct future-from-European-past extrapolation. This scoped mechanism claim supplies no universal historical sequence.',[tw])],'Read primary1985chapter169–172; original book bodies and full comparative datasets remain unread. Original title and later revision are distinguished.',[])
life('charles_tilly','deceased',[(tl,'Opening death statement; late biography paragraph','Institutional obituary states deathApril29,2008 and birthMay27,1929.')],1929,2008,precise_death='2008-04-29',limits='Birth retained at year precision; supported death day is institutional. Some external memorials differ on birth day; no reconciliation is asserted here.')

