gl=source('goody_life','Royal Anthropological Institute, Jack Goody obituary.','https://therai.org.uk/archives-and-manuscripts/obituaries/jack-goody/','Read opening obituary: SirJohnRankine(Jack)Goody born27July1919,died16July2015; biographical context matches Cambridge scholar.',True)
bio('jack_goody','Jack Goody brought anthropological comparison to histories of kinship, literacy and Eurasian societies. His jointly authored work with Ian Watt examined literacy; The Development of the Family and Marriage in Europe questioned claims about uniquely European family patterns while distinguishing kinship from demographic measures. The Theft of History criticizes accounts that make European experience the template for universal historical development. His comparisons seek similarities and differences across societies; they do not establish a single timeless family system or an undifferentiated East.',[event(1963,'With Ian Watt,The Consequences of Literacy',[gd],'Joint article in ComparativeStudiesinSocietyandHistory5.3(April1963),304–345; not2009digital publication.'),event(1983,'The Development of the Family and Marriage in Europe',[gf],'Publisher preview imprint confirms1983; selected8–9read, not entirebook.'),event(2006,'The Theft of History',[gt,gm],'OriginalCambridge2006;laterdigital postingdates notredated contribution.')],[edge('demography','Questions the uniqueness and uniformity of European family patterns','Primary8–9 distinguishes kinship questions from demographic measures, stresses regional/social variation and considers Tibet/RomanEgypt counterexamples. The cited studies themselves were not independently checked.',[gf],'critique'),edge('global','Challenges Europe-centred templates for world historical comparison','Introduction1–3 criticizes projecting European categories onto other societies and asks why Eurasian similarities disappear from divergence narratives. His argument is represented as a comparative challenge, not a proven global consensus.',[gt])],'Read TheFamilyandMarriage8–9 includingnote1 and TheTheftIntroduction1–3. Watt joint credit and original/digital dates remain distinct.',[('global','Uses anthropological comparison to question Europe-centred accounts of Eurasian development.','The Theft of History(2006),Introduction1–3',[gt],1)],{gf:{'status':'supporting_page_checked','checked_on':DAY,'note':'Additional188review: reread printed8–9 includingnote1 from publisher preview. Scope remains kinship/demography distinctions, regional/social variation and qualified challenge to uniqueness; cited empirical studies not independently checked. Entirebookunread.'}})
