pc=source('chatterjee_interview','Partha Chatterjee and Ayça Çubukçu, “Empire as a Practice of Power:An Interview,” Asia-PacificJournal10.41.1(8October2012),3840.','https://apjjf.org/2012/10/41/partha-chatterjee/3840/article','Read primary interview printed2–3 via Cambridge-hostedPDF,doi10.1017/S1557466012033256. Original2012date retained despite2025digitalmigration. Chatterjee describes combining localnarrative/conceptualhistory and learning fromSubalternStudies. His sweeping claim about what no intellectualhistorian studies is not adopted.')
pb=source('chatterjee_books','Columbia Anthropology, Partha Chatterjee biography and bibliography.','https://anthropology.columbia.edu/content/partha-chatterjee','Read biography and named-book dates1986/1993/2012. Politicaltheory, anthropology and historian roles retained; metadata does not establish an influence claim.',True)
pl=source('chatterjee_life','Partha Chatterjee, “Populism and Fascism:What’s the Difference?” The Book Review50.5(May2026).','https://www.thebookreviewindia.org/populism-and-fascism-whats-the-difference/','Read byline,May2026issue and first-person opening memorial-lecture statement. Positive new author/lecture evidence, matched to Columbia scholar; later politicalclaims not used for graphrelationships.',True)
bio('partha_chatterjee','Partha Chatterjee studies colonial nationalism and the changing practices of imperial power. His work joins political theory and anthropology to historical research on colonial and postcolonial India. In his interview about The Black Hole of Empire, he explains how a local story of Calcutta and its memorial becomes the spine of a wider inquiry into concepts and practices of empire. He also identifies a lesson from Subaltern Studies: popular cultural forms can be analysed alongside elite political discourse. These are selective historical methods rather than a claim that one account exhausts empire.',[event(1986,'Nationalist Thought and the Colonial World:A Derivative Discourse?',[pb,'chatterjee'],'Original1986publication;colonialnationalismhistoricalproject, fullbookunread.'),event(1993,'The Nation and Its Fragments:Colonial and Postcolonial Histories',[pb,'chatterjee_fragments'],'OriginalPrinceton1993;previousboundedprimarychapterreview preserved, not newly claimed wholebookreading.'),event(2012,'The Black Hole of Empire:History of a Global Practice of Power',[pc,pb],'Princeton2012;selected2012authorinterview not2025Cambridgemigration date.')],[edge('global','Braids a local imperial narrative with the history of governing concepts and practices','Interview2–3 explains how FortWilliam and the BlackHolestory connect with selectively traced politicaleconomy,law and imperialtechniques. He explicitly notes the selection required; no completeglobalempirehistory is inferred.',[pc]),edge('subaltern','Brings popular cultural practices into the historical analysis of imperial power','Interview2 says he learned throughSubalternStudies to interrogate popularculture theoretically, using theatre and football alongside policy/intellectualdiscourse. His claim about otherhistorians’ omissions is not adopted.',[pc])],'Read interview2–3 only; remaininginterview and completebooksunread. Existing1993chapter andAndersonexchange sourcequalifications remain unchanged.',[('global','Connects selected local colonial narratives with wider concepts and practices of imperial power.','The Black Hole of Empire(2012);Çubukçuinterview2–3',[pc],0)])
life('partha_chatterjee','living',[(pl,'May2026byline and opening first-person lecture statement','Positive author testimony to delivering the current memoriallecture; distinct from politician and SNUeconomist namesakes.')],limits='Positive2026author/lecture evidence establishes status in the checked source, with no claim to day-by-day verification throughOctober25. Columbia biography matches the historicalscholar.')

# Alessandro Portelli.
